ng either note: select o.observation_id, o.surface_form from observed_terms o join term_adjudications a using (observation_id) where a.outcome=''DEFERRED'' order by 1. || CORRECTED 2026-09-25: OWNER RULING 2026-09-25, AND WHAT IT DID NOT SETTLE. TERM-089 (''ramp'') is narrowed to exclude the running slope of paths, walkways and trails, so it no longer pre-decides group (1), the ramp-versus-route question, which stays OPEN here. THREE FURTHER POINTS that an independent adversarial pass raised on batch 20''s minted terms (finding #14) are NOT resolved by that ruling and are open on this gap: (a) TERM-094 (''accessibility audit'') has a scope note that states as a universal -- ''An audit measures COMPLIANCE with a yardstick, never what the yardstick should be'' -- what is one testable prior (batch 20''s P1b) drawn from two sources; (b) TERM-093 (''ramp usability'') is defined as ''the outcome a ramp gradient is judged against'' and its scope note adjudicates the measures to it as operationalisations, which forecloses a separate term for the acceptability CRITERION as distinct from the measure; (c) TERM-092''s scope note says ''It bounds TERM ramp run length from above'' -- a dangling reference with no term id. Each is a vocabulary decision for the owner, not a wording fix, and none was edited. || CORRECTED 2026-09-28: INVESTIGATED, batch 21, item 1 (ramp-versus-route scope of parameter 3) ONLY -- NOT RESOLVED, deliberately: this is the owner''s call per GAP-047''s own ruling (''The ruling covers TERM-089 only ... left for the batch that re-determines parameter 3''), and this batch does not re-determine it. THE FULL AFFECTED SET, derived rather than guessed: select extraction_id, ref_id, claim_text from source_value_extractions where parameter_id=3 and (claim_text like ''%path%'' or claim_text like ''%trail%'' or claim_text like ''%walkway%'') -- gives extraction_id 39 (REF-00977, a ranking phrase naming ''pathway characteristics'' among least-accessible elements generally, no path-specific gradient VALUE -- the weakest case for reclassification), 43 (REF-01001, gravel-trail gradient, 9.01% threshold), 49 (REF-01003, outdoor-pathway difficulty ratings). And on the term-adjudication side: select observation_id, ref_id, surface_form from observed_terms o join term_adjudications a using(observation_id) where a.outcome=''DEFERRED'' and a.rationale like ''%ramp-versus-route boundary is unresolved%'' -- gives observations 40, 41 (REF-01001, trail-slope threshold and usability category) and 47, 48 (REF-01003, path running-slope and perceived-difficulty), all four carrying byte-identical boilerplate rationale, all blocked on this same decision. TWO OPTIONS, NOT A RECOMMENDATION: (A) NARROW -- parameter 3 stays element-scoped to engineered ramps only, consistent with TERM-089''s own 2026-09-25 narrowing; extractions 43 and 49 (and arguably 39) move to a new route/path running-slope parameter once minted, and observations 40/41/47/48 adjudicate to that new term. (B) BROAD -- parameter 3 is a FUNCTIONAL/OUTCOME parameter (the maximum climbable running gradient anywhere on an accessible route, however constructed), for which the ramp/path distinction is a construction-method detail the determination does not care about; under this reading extractions 43/49 are correctly filed as-is and only TERM-001''s own name/scope-note may need broadening to stop implying a narrower scope than parameter 3 actually serves (worth noting: ADA 2010''s own definition of ''ramp'' is functional -- ''a walking surface with a running slope steeper than 1:20'' -- which is evidence FOR reading B, not a settlement of it). Neither option is applied; extraction 49 (and 39, 43) are UNCHANGED by this batch.', "updated_at" = '2026-09-28 06:59', "updated_by_session" = 'session_2026-09-28-research-batch-21-selection' WHERE "gap_id" = 'GAP-033';
UPDATE "gaps" SET "description" = 'TWO WRITER-REACH DEFECTS FOUND BY A CODE REVIEW OF BATCH 19, both narrower than they look and both leaving a column no sanctioned verb can fix. (1) journal_name AND publisher ARE NOW CORRECTABLE ONLY ON DOI-BEARING ROWS. Batch 19 added them to db.py _CORRECTABLE so GAP-031 could be discharged, and extended retrieval_log _BIBLIO_FIELDS so the verifier can actually check them -- which restores the invariant _CORRECTABLE header states. BUT correct-source keys its payload BY DOI (db.py: "no DOI, so no payload can be keyed to it"), and amend-source refuses anything in _CORRECTABLE by design. So for a row with no DOI both columns are now unreachable by EVERY sanctioned writer. Derive the exposure rather than quoting it: select count(*) from evidence_sources where coalesce(doi,"")="" -- and note WHICH rows those are, because the bite is precise: a REPORT is the source type whose publisher matters most and the one least likely to carry a DOI. REF-01005, this batch own 1979 HUD report, is exactly that case. THE FIX IS TO KEY THE PAYLOAD BY SOMETHING OTHER THAN A DOI. retrieval_log already resolves locators through several columns for verify_authors (LOCATOR_COLUMNS), and correct-source could take the same route -- url, handle, report_number -- instead of DOI alone. Not attempted in batch 19 because it changes how every correction finds its payload and deserves its own change. (2) PAGE LOCATORS DO NOT DECLARE THEIR NUMBERING, AND THREE ARE IN USE. source_value_extractions.locator_scheme takes the value "page" and nothing says WHICH page: the zero-based index pypdf and pymupdf use, the one-based PDF page a viewer shows, or the number PRINTED on the sheet. For REF-01005 those are 162, 163 and 161 for the same page. Batch 19 wrote 163 on extraction 57 and then, correcting itself, called it "the PDF page index", which is the one thing it is not; extraction 54 from batch 18 carries the same number for the same sheet. A reader holding the physical report is sent to the wrong page, and a reader re-deriving with pypdf is sent to a different wrong page. scripts/research/page_image.py speaks only zero-based indices and says so in its output, which is a start; the schema still does not. CHEAPEST FIX: declare the convention in locator_scheme vocabulary itself -- page_pdf1, page_pdf0, page_printed -- so the column carries the answer instead of a convention nobody wrote down. || CORRECTED 2026-09-28: EXTENDED, batch 21 (session_2026-09-28-research-batch-21-selection), with a concrete instance and a second, related sub-defect this batch found and briefly mis-filed as a new gap (GAP-054, closed as a duplicate of this one by an independent adversarial pass). CONCRETE INSTANCE OF (1): REF-01010 (a DOI-less statutory instrument) needed --pages set at add-source time and was not; amend-source refuses it (bibliographic field) and correct-source refuses it (no DOI to key a payload to) -- exactly item (1)''s mechanism, now for pages/article_number rather than journal_name/publisher. RELATED, NOT YET STATED IN (1): research_batch_dod''s R3 check (clause/section/page citation on regulatory-stratum sources) reads evidence_sources.pages/article_number specifically -- never source_value_extractions.loc_section/loc_subsection/loc_clause, which REF-01010''s own extraction (parameter 3, MOB, 1:12 gradient) DOES carry precisely (loc_section=12, loc_subsection=3, loc_clause=a), byte-verified against the persisted primary PDF (page 15 of 42). So a source can be genuinely, precisely clause-cited at the extraction level and still fail R3 forever, for every DOI-less regulatory/statutory/code source this project ever admits mis-keyed this way -- with no remediation path except getting --pages right on the FIRST add-source call. A THIRD, NARROWER CASE found the same batch: correct-source''s own refusal to write a field the payload states nothing for (''a silence is not a correction'') means even a DOI-HAVING row can end up permanently uncorrectable if a field was originally mis-filed with a value the payload does not carry at all -- REF-01009 stores article-number 237 in  (now duplicated correctly into  too via correct-source, but the wrong  value cannot be nulled by any writer: amend-source refuses it as bibliographic, correct-source refuses to null a field its payload is silent on). WHAT WOULD CLOSE ITEM (1) is unchanged from GAP-043''s own text (key the payload by url/handle/report_number as well as DOI, per LOCATOR_COLUMNS). WHAT WOULD ALSO HELP: either R3 accepting source_value_extractions.loc_* as an alternative satisfying condition, or correct-source gaining an explicit ''set NULL because the payload contradicts the stored value'' mode distinct from its current ''refuse rather than guess'' one. Not remediated this batch; recorded as two reasoned waivers (REF-01010.pages unset, REF-01009.pages left at the wrong value 237 alongside the now-correct article_number=237). || CORRECTED 2026-09-28: CORRECTION TO THE IMMEDIATELY PRECEDING NOTE, same session, self-caught: that note was written via a shell command that put the column names pages and article_number inside backtick characters, which bash interpreted as command substitution ($(pages), $(article_number)) rather than literal text -- both evaluated to empty output, silently deleting those two words from three places in the note. The intended sentence read: "REF-01009 stores article-number 237 in pages (now duplicated correctly into article_number too via correct-source, but the wrong pages value cannot be nulled by any writer: amend-source refuses it as bibliographic, correct-source refuses to null a field its payload is silent on)." The garbled text is left in place above (append-only); this is the correction.', "updated_at" = '2026-09-28 07:37', "updated_by_session" = 'session_2026-09-28-research-batch-21-selection' WHERE "gap_id" = 'GAP-043';
UPDATE "gaps" SET "description" = 'BATCH 20''s R1 (Co-1 / T2) LEG WAS A THIN DISCHARGE OF R1, NOT A FULL ONE. It ran three queries (exec 92-94): English only, one biomedical full-text index (Europe PMC), no DPO or disability-organisation channel, no non-English vocabulary, no grey-literature route. research_batch_dod R1 passes when Co-1-targeted searches exist, which is why the gate was green; an independent adversarial pass (finding #20) sustained that the leg did not do what R1 exists for -- ''Co-1 / T2 / Co-2 pass FIRST'', co-primary with T1 under CRPD 4.3. FOR A FUTURE BATCH, not a repair: a Co-1 pass on ramp gradient that reaches DPO and user-led outputs in more than one language and more than one kind of index. Candidate 126 (Mactaggart 2024, a participatory audit staged by this leg) is the strongest lead it produced. || CORRECTED 2026-09-28: ATTEMPTED, batch 21 (session_2026-09-28-research-batch-21-selection, exec_id 101-104). Four web-engine searches (not biomedical-index), two languages (ES, FR), each pass generic-technical-query then DPO-name-anchored (CERMI; APF France handicap): all four surfaced only commercial ramp-equipment vendors and construction-compliance blogs restating national code figures (Spain''s CTE DB-SUA 10/8/6%, France''s 2014/2017 arrêtés 5/8/10/12%), never a DPO''s own voice -- ASPAYM (a genuine Spanish disability-support association) came closest but its own ramp page is itself a CTE restatement, not lived-experience material. R14: this is NOT a query-shape failure -- both a generic and a DPO-name-anchored query shape were tried in each language -- so the honest reading is that ramp-GRADIENT specifically (as opposed to disability rights broadly) is not where these two DPOs'' indexed web content concentrates, at least not reachable by a general web search engine. GAP-049 remains OPEN: the underlying weakness (thin Co-1/DPO coverage on this parameter) is not resolved, only further evidenced with two additional honestly-null language/DPO combinations. A future attempt should try a channel other than general web search -- a DPO''s own document repository/publications page directly, or a disability-studies-specific index -- rather than repeating this query shape in a third language. SEPARATE CAUTION recorded on exec_id 104''s findings_note: the search tool''s own generated summary asserted an untraceable named testimony and report citation that was NOT used anywhere in this batch -- see that row. || CORRECTED 2026-09-28: CORRECTION, caught by an independent adversarial pass, not self-caught. The prior note''s claim that this pass found ''never a DPO''s own voice'' and was an ''honestly null'' result is WRONG: the CERMI-anchored search (exec 103) DID surface a genuinely DPO-co-branded document -- the Madrid city council / CERMI Comunidad de Madrid joint accessibility methodology (Dec 2018, CERMI''s name on the title page and every page footer) -- which was retrieved, read, and quoted in exec_id=105''s own findings_note, with real parameter-3 longitudinal-slope figures (10%/8% by ramp length) and a cross-slope figure (2%). It was simply never staged as a search_candidates row despite prose twice asserting it had been -- an R7 filing failure, now corrected (candidate_id=132), not a genuine absence of DPO material. R14''s classification in the prior note (''NOT a query-shape failure... genuine absence'') is WRONG for this document specifically: the shape worked, the filing did not happen. GAP-049 stays OPEN on its own terms regardless (whether CERMI''s role in this document is genuine co-production per D-0178, versus a municipal document merely co-branded, is unresolved -- see candidate_id=132''s why_not_admitted), but the batch''s own self-assessment of its search yield was overstated in the negative direction, which is worth recording as plainly as an overstated positive would be.', "updated_at" = '2026-09-28 07:34', "updated_by_session" = 'session_2026-09-28-research-batch-21-selection' WHERE "gap_id" = 'GAP-049';

