UPDATE "search_candidates" SET "disposition" = 'ADMITTED', "notes" = 'WHAT IT SAYS ON RAMPS, VERBATIM FROM THE PERSISTED FULL TEXT: method -- ''For long ramps (over 2 m), 2-3 measurements were taken at various points along the length, and the steepest angle was recorded''; finding -- ''of three facilities with ramps, all reached the minimum required width and had textured, non-slip floors but two were too steep and none had handrails, meaning all failed the overall accessibility criteria for a ramp.'' Five facilities audited, three had ramps. R7: a failure finding. Same cluster as candidates 123/124 (health-facility audits in LMICs) and REF-01006, and the second participatory-audit design found in two batches. NEXT: retrieve Supplementary File Appendix 2 from the publisher (BMC additional files) and read the ramp indicator''s slope criterion. || RESOLVED 2026-09-25 (R15, re-described from the source): CORRECTION TO THIS ROW''S WARRANT DESCRIPTION (independent adversarial pass, finding #13). It says the audits were co-facilitated by youth researchers with disabilities, as though that settled the Co-1 question for this cell. The persisted text: the eight youth researchers who adapted the tool were ''themselves all Ugandan healthcare users with disabilities''; ''Six of the eight youth researchers were selected to participate in the pilot as trainee facilitators''; ''Each pair of trainee facilitators included one youth researcher with a disability and one peer without a disability. One trainee was Deaf, one lived with albinism, and one had visual impairment.'' NO MOBILITY IMPAIRMENT IS RECORDED among the facilitators. For staging against parameter 3 x MOB that matters: whatever Co-1 warrant the co-production carries under D-0178, it is not co-production by the population this parameter serves. || RESOLVED 2026-09-28 by session_2026-09-28-research-batch-21-selection: R15, re-described from the source; admitted as REF-01009: Supplementary File Appendix 1 (Accessibility Standards Minimum Criteria) retrieved and read: the composite ''Ramp meets access standards'' indicator requires slope <=1:12, width >=1300mm, handrails, and a non-slip surface, footnoted against two cited sources -- a 2014 Uganda ''practical guide'' (recommending the gentler 1:20, or 1:10 for very short ramps) and the binding Building Control (Accessibility Standards for Persons with Disabilities) Code, 2019 (max 1:12, clause 12(3)(a)). The latter is independently admitted as REF-01010 with its own extraction. Supplementary Table 2 corroborates the main text''s failure finding at facility-aggregate level: ''Ramp meets access standards'' = 0 of 3 facilities with a ramp (0%). D-0178 READING (per this candidate''s own flagged open question): genuine co-production, not mere data-collection labour -- the eight disabled youth researchers reviewed and proposed changes to the DAC''s individual questions (Round 1) and held a round-table review of the draft adapted tool with the study team (Round 3), i.e. they shaped the instrument''s content, not just administered it. Admitted at evidence_type=co1, tier=1. R13: no mobility impairment is named among the eight (Deaf/albinism/visual-impairment are the three individually named), so parameter-3 x MOB is graded PROXY, not matched -- recorded on extraction_id=71.', "resolved_ref_id" = 'REF-01009', "updated_at" = '2026-09-28 06:48', "updated_by_session" = 'session_2026-09-28-research-batch-21-selection' WHERE "candidate_id" = 126;
